ced when you're  in the sort of sea area european common  european framework which by the way i  think is very good and i will leave the  reference to wikipedia  here  uh if you see that what the how they  describe the advanced person  c1 can understand a wide range of  demanding longer texts and recognize  implicit meaning can express him herself  fluently and spontaneously without much  obvious searching for expressions can  use language flexibly and effectively  for social academic and professional  purposes can produce clear  well-structured detailed text here we're  talking writing again on complex  subjects showing controlled use of  organizational patterns connectors and  cohesive devices this is c1 so this is  the first level of advanced  and then just to finish off c2 which is  the ultimate  target which i don't think many people  uh achieve and i don't think that  possibly i'm there in french because i  studied in france for three years but uh  c2 can understand with ease virtually  everything heard or read can summarize  information from different spoken and  written sources reconstructing arguments  and accounts in a coherent presentation  can express him herself spontaneously  very fluently and precisely  differentiating finer shades of meaning  even in the most complex situations all  right  so  what we see here is that that there  should be a difference between  fluency in terms of flowing  comfort level within a limited range of  subject matter  and advanced learners so  can we get fluent in three months it  depends what your goals are  i think a lot of people when they learn  languages i think a lot of people just  want to be comfortable talking to the  people that they'll meet on their next  trip to mexico  so that can be achieved i think fairly  quickly  i think a lot of  more ambitious learners myself included  academic learners would like to achieve  you'd like to get into that c1c2 area  they would like to be  essentially as  comfortable in the language as an  educated  native speaker  so how do you get there you don't get  there just by talking and a lot of  people i know it's quite common even in  japan people want to go to their ego  eikaiwa class and they just want to talk  and it's my belief and i think this is  ultimately the what i think is the most  important point in this whole discussion  and that is that  if your goal is to achieve this  uh degree of of even the b2 the advanced  intermediate level  you have to do a lot of reading  a lot of reading and and i like  combining reading with listening  because after all  words were originally spoken before they  were written down  and and so and i think we learn better  if we do both reading and listening so i  like to  always have both the audio and the text  available uh  plus  uh a listing is a lot easier to do i can  do it whenever i want i can do it while  i'm doing other tasks reading is more of  a dedicated task  and if we look at what happens with  native speakers  it has been proven through research that  those people who read a lot  do better nothing tracks  to job performance and academic  performance as much as  how literate people are and you know  they divide the native speaking  population into five  sort of levels of literacy the top 20  percent the second twenty percent and so  forth uh  apparently forty percent of the  population in north america and in most  countries in europe  uh don't read well enough to perform  effectively in a modern information  society now the top 20  of people who read the most and the best  they do the best professionally  academically so if that's the case for  people in their native language i think  it has to be equally the case uh when we  are talking about learning  the language that those people use and  and wanting to perform at the level that  the educated person performs at in that  language  uh it's furthermore i i want to  emphasize that the connection between  listening and reading  uh they have found that when kids learn  to read in school initially they all  kind of learn at the same speed those  that  you know don't have any particular you  know dyslexia or other problems because  they learn to decode at the same time  and all of a sudden they can decode  these words however by grade three or  grade four all of a sudden there's a  great  uh separation that takes place and those  kids who grew up in families  where the parents spoke to them on a  variety of subjects  they have a bigger vocabulary and  therefore they their reading takes off  because they know a lot of words as they  decode they discover these words and of  course the more words that you know in a  context the more you're able to infer  meaning and  and guess at the meaning of of new words  so that it's a real example of the rich  get richer and the poor get poorer now  an adult learner of course  uh  trying to catch up with the native  speakers a bit behind the eight ball so  to speak is at a disadvantage however  the adult learner if the adult learner  is well read in his or her own language  that adult learner will have an easier  time acquiring vocabulary in the new  language if you aren't even familiar  with the concept the word in your own  language you'll have an awfully tough  time learning it in the new language so  what i want to stress here is that to  get to these levels of c1 and c2  you have to do a lot of reading and the  listening in a in effect supports  reading  uh because what you're doing is you're  exposing yourself to words  and if your focus is entirely just on  speaking you will necessarily stay  within a limited range  of vocabulary because you'll presumably  meet with the same people talk about  those things that you're comfortable  talking about  and you will pick up some additional  vocabulary as you hear them speak but it  won't be as effective  in increasing your vocabulary as massive  input so what i want to end up with here  is the the suggestion that if people are  serious about reaching a high level in  the language c1 c2  massive listening and reading is going  to give you the base  from which you can then develop the  speaking skills and the writing skills  which obviously have to be worked as  well but without the base of words  without the base of vocabulary without  the exposure to the language through  massive listening and reading you will  not be able to develop that  ability to produce the language because  you won't have the words and i think  that in all of this discussion about  fluent in three months or what can you  achieve  um  the key thing is let's not forget  that there are no shortcuts  that in order to achieve the level that  i described as c1 and c2 you have to  invest a lot of time in listening and  reading  so thank you very much and  i look forward to comments and questions  bye for

